            <ArticleTitle>Muscle activation patterns in subjects with and without low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>816-21</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To analyze muscle activation patterns during various footplate perturbations, used as proprioceptive challenges in patients with low back pain (LBP) and in controls.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A prospective and controlled comparative study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatient clinic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty subjects with chronic LBP and 20 age- and sex-matched controls.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The subjects underwent 5 sets of footplate perturbations in 3 directions with 16 perturbations for each set.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Latency, frequency, and asymmetry of muscle activation of the erector spinae, rectus abdominus, anterior tibialis, and gastrocnemius muscles were measured bilaterally with surface electromyography.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In the toes-up movements, subjects with LBP were significantly less likely to activate their rectus abdominus muscles (P=.02), and they were more likely to exhibit asymmetric muscle activation in the smaller forward movements (odds ratio=4.1, P=.03). The latter result appears to be driven by asymmetric contraction of the erector spinae and rectus abdominus.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Significantly more subjects with LBP than control subjects exhibited absent firing of trunk muscles during 2 of the 5 footplate perturbations. These results suggest an abnormality of the neuromuscular loop and may represent altered proprioception.</AbstractText>
